@startuml neural-pseudo-perplexity
' MLM pseudo-perplexity of one sentence: T masked copies, T forward passes, one gathered
' log-probability each. Shows exactly which tensor leaves which stage, and marks the cost.
' Render: plantuml -tsvg docs/diagrams/neural-pseudo-perplexity.puml
' Source of truth for Figure 2 in docs/components/neural/rescorer.md §4.
skinparam backgroundColor #FAFAFA
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultTextAlignment center
skinparam ArrowColor #37474F
skinparam ArrowFontColor #37474F
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor #37474F
  LifeLineBorderColor #90A4AE
  LifeLineBackgroundColor #ECEFF1
  ParticipantBorderColor #263238
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #2E7D32
  ParticipantFontColor white
  ParticipantFontStyle bold
  ActorBorderColor #263238
}
title MLM pseudo-perplexity — mask every position once  (Salazar et al., 2020)

participant "ModernBertRescorer\n::pseudo_perplexity" as R
participant "Tokenizer" as T
participant "ModernBertModel\n::get_mlm_logits" as M
participant "candle_nn::ops\n::log_softmax" as S

R -> T : encode(sentence, add_special = true)
T --> R : W = (w₁ … w_T),  T tokens\n(including [CLS] and [SEP])

R -> T : token_to_id("[MASK]")
T --> R : mask_id = 50 284\n(NeuralError::Tokenization if absent)

loop for each position t = 1 … T
  R -> R : W∖t ← clone(W);  W∖t[t] ← [MASK]
  R -> M : input_ids = W∖t   (shape 1 × T)
  note right of M #E8F5E9
    encoder → MLM head → tied decoder
    logits Z ∈ ℝ^(1 × T × 50 368)
  end note
  M --> R : Z
  R -> R : z_t ← Z[0, t, ·]   (one row: ℝ^50368)
  R -> S : log_softmax(z_t, dim = −1)
  S --> R : log ℙ(· | W∖t)
  R -> R : **accumulate log ℙ(w_t | W∖t)**\n(the //original// token, not the argmax)
end

R -> R : PLL(W) = Σₜ log ℙ(w_t | W∖t)
R -> R : **PPPL(W) = exp( −PLL(W) / T )**

note over R, S #FFF8E1
  **Cost.** T forward passes for a T-token sentence — the masked copies are
  //not// batched into one tensor. With B = batch_size this is still T sequential
  encoder calls per sentence; batch_size only chunks the //loop over paths// in
  rescore_paths. Scoring an n-token beam of width k costs Θ(k · T) encoder passes.
end note

note over R #FFF8E1
  **Divergence from the reference.** Salazar et al. score only the //content//
  tokens; this implementation masks [CLS] and [SEP] too, so T counts them and
  their near-deterministic (high) log-probabilities enter the mean, pulling PPPL
  toward 1. They are 2 terms out of T, so the pull is //stronger for shorter
  sentences//: PPPL here is not comparable to published numbers, and comparing
  candidates of **different lengths** carries a length-dependent bias. Beams whose
  candidates are all the same length are unaffected in //rank//.
end note

@enduml
